      "latency_ms": 16358.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1176,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen is, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules lock into a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to carry nutrients and waste throughout the body and move minerals through the soil.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the **primary component of all known forms of life**. It is necessary for:\n*   **Cellular function:** Chemical reactions inside cells require a watery environment.\n*   **Transport:** It acts as a medium to move nutrients and oxygen to cells and remove waste.\n*   **Temperature regulation:** Through sweating and panting, animals use water to cool down."
